WEBVTT

1
00:56:42.530 --> 00:56:54.530
Do you all understand me? I am Rikki, for those who do not know me. I have been Paul’s best friend for over 35 years, and I want to tell you something about him.

2
00:56:54.530 --> 00:57:13.530
Behind the tears of sorrow lies the smile of memories. I met Paul over 35 years ago at the tanning salon, through Kees, a former schoolmate of his, with whom I had more or less a relationship.

3
00:57:13.530 --> 00:57:33.530
Kees and Paul were completely different. Kees was very full of himself, extravagant, Mr. Hollywood, and incredibly vain. Paul was down to earth, a cheerful chatterbox, and interested in his fellow man.

4
00:57:33.530 --> 00:57:54.530
I found myself in an unpleasant, suppressed relationship during the eccentric phase, where I went through life like a sort of sanderman. I wore all black clothing layered over each other, a lot of jewelry, and I didn’t do this under the influence of the E-team.

5
00:57:54.530 --> 00:58:17.530
And as a cherry on top, I wore a sanderman coat that swirled behind me. In that respect, we were not at all each other's type. Yet, we started talking more and more at the tanning salon. Eventually, this led to dinners and nights out.

6
00:58:17.530 --> 00:58:27.530
Kees, who had a somewhat oversized ego after my speech, was now out of the picture. This was to Paul's great delight, as Paul never really liked Kees.

7
00:58:27.530 --> 00:58:43.530
One of the first times I went out with Paul, he said, if you go out with me, you have to drink a bit. Well, I found out what that meant.

8
00:58:44.530 --> 00:59:03.530
We went to the Repelsteel and it was happy hour, so you got two drinks for the price of one. At that moment, I was drinking safari juice, palbier. After just a few safaris, I really didn’t feel so good. Paul said, you know what, you should eat something, lay a foundation, and then we can continue.

9
00:59:03.530 --> 00:59:16.530
I said okay, so we went to a shawarma place, I ordered a falafel sandwich, but that sandwich hadn’t even arrived in front of me before I was already face down in the sambal. He teased me about that for years.

10
00:59:17.530 --> 00:59:26.530
That drinking thing has stayed with me, but I stuck to fresh juice, because that suited me much better.

11
00:59:33.530 --> 00:59:43.530
How Paul managed to drink all that is a mystery, because he was just back the next day putting tires on the car. No problem at all.

12
00:59:44.530 --> 01:00:00.530
On weekends, I always had night shifts. Often, he would come pick me up and we would have breakfast at Motel Eindhoven.

13
01:00:00.530 --> 01:00:12.530
At that time, you could have breakfast there; it was a very large breakfast buffet. Paul and I really loved food, so we would go there in the mornings.

14
01:00:13.530 --> 01:00:20.530
In winter, he would come pick me up in his Datsun, a very small car, an old wreck with a broken heating system.

15
01:00:21.530 --> 01:00:29.530
There was just a tiny hole to look through to see where he was driving. It’s a miracle we made it to our destination.

16
01:00:30.530 --> 01:00:37.530
We did the breakfast buffet quite often. We really ate a lot; we just lingered there.

17
01:00:38.530 --> 01:00:46.530
So before long, the rate had gone up. Whether that was convenient for us, we never knew, but we certainly enjoyed it.

18
01:00:46.530 --> 01:01:03.530
When we went out, I was usually dancing, and Paul loved to chat, so he would engage in conversations with everyone, especially with women.

19
01:01:04.530 --> 01:01:11.530
I was always fascinated by how he could wrap those women around his finger. They were always completely impressed by him.

20
01:01:11.530 --> 01:01:24.530
He thought it was absolutely great. He didn’t always take them home, not at all, but he just enjoyed chatting with them. In that respect, he was always perfectly content.

21
01:01:24.530 --> 01:01:45.530
At one point, I was on the night shift and then I got a call from my downstairs neighbor with the message that if you don't come and get your dog right now, I will put your dog out on the street.

22
01:01:46.530 --> 01:02:00.530
You should know, dogs are my life. Dogs are my everything. What is above for Paul, my dogs are for me. They really stand on a very high pedestal.

23
01:02:00.530 --> 01:02:10.530
So I was in complete panic because I was working at the Weerde, I was a night nurse, I was on duty alone, I couldn't just leave. So I thought, oh what about my dog.

24
01:02:11.530 --> 01:02:21.530
So I called Paul, I said, god Paul, can you go to my apartment because this and that is the case. Well, he immediately jumped in the car, picked up the keys, and went to my apartment.

25
01:02:22.530 --> 01:02:35.530
He found her douchie, that was my dog's name, completely in a panic. I had just gotten that dog because someone had thrown it into my arms. It couldn't go anywhere, so I thought, well, it can come to me.

26
01:02:35.530 --> 01:02:52.530
Because where I lived, you weren't allowed to have dogs at all. So that was actually the problem. So he went to the apartment, took care of the dog. Yes, he said, he has peed all over the house. Out of panic, he said.

27
01:02:52.530 --> 01:03:07.530
But yes, with Paul, he could also exaggerate everything. He could blow everything a bit out of proportion. So you always had to take him with a grain of salt. That was generally the case. Except for himself, of course, with his illness and so on.

28
01:03:07.530 --> 01:03:21.530
Or if he had something, he had a broken arm. Or I fell and broke something on his nose. Oh, that will be fine, he would say. No problem. But yes, that exaggeration is also very characteristic of Paul.

29
01:03:21.530 --> 01:03:45.530
But over the years, I managed to keep that a bit in balance. In any case, I thought, if he says it like that, then it is so. But he did it anyway. Because Paul didn't have much to do with dogs. But he knew very well that I did.

30
01:03:45.530 --> 01:04:09.530
And I think that from that time on, before I might have taken Paul for granted, just having fun and this and that. But then I realized that he was really a very good friend. Because if you do something for my dogs, that means a lot. And he always did that.

31
01:04:09.530 --> 01:04:32.530
When Paul met Anita, he entered calmer waters. He thought more and more about the future. He had a more stable life; before that, it was all steps and Hallelujah. And she was also the first girlfriend he really introduced to me. I had a good feeling about it right away.

32
01:04:40.530 --> 01:04:42.530
Sorry, I need a moment.

33
01:04:43.530 --> 01:05:02.530
The Step Evenings were reduced, that had been the case for a while. But they were converted into concerts and theater visits. We went to see comedians very often, sometimes to the theater. A lot of concerts by De Dijk and I don't know where else we went.

34
01:05:03.530 --> 01:05:16.530
Paul, do you know where we should go? We need to go to Herbie Hancock. You know, from that hit of papap, papap, paparap, papararap. That's really fun. Okay, said Paul, then let's go.

35
01:05:16.530 --> 01:05:34.530
Well, we went there. We were in that hall. We looked around us. All very serious people. And Herbie Hancock, who had that hit, turned out to be a very heavy jazz musician. But really heavy jazz.

36
01:05:34.530 --> 01:05:57.530
So really for advanced listeners. I had expected a completely different setting, so I couldn't hold back my laughter. I really got the giggles and Paul kept pushing me the whole time. And he was so embarrassed. Eventually, the break came, we went out and we really couldn't stop laughing.

37
01:05:58.530 --> 01:06:14.530
One time we also went to see BB King in Antwerp. Paul said, no, you know, we'll go from Lommel, because that's closer and I know the way well and it will all be fine. So I drove to him, from Lommel to Antwerp. The outward journey went reasonably well, all back roads.

38
01:06:14.530 --> 01:06:32.530
Well, the concert was really perfect, it was really amazing. Then we went back, completely lost. We took over four hours for that trip and eventually, I got home after six hours and then I had to walk the dogs again the next morning.

39
01:06:32.530 --> 01:06:53.530
That return trip was not such a success, but Paul could always bluff a bit. That bluffing has actually brought him a lot. Paul lived like Pippi Longstocking. His motto was, I've never done it before, so I think I can do it.

40
01:06:53.530 --> 01:07:08.530
That's how he became a cook, that's how he became a painter, that's how he ended up behind the bar, that's how he became a masseur. Yes, that's how it went and he was good at it. Except for that one time he wanted to show me how to polish a glass.

41
01:07:08.530 --> 01:07:32.530
He said, Rikie, I'm going to teach you how to polish a glass. I had these really beautiful crystal glasses, well he started with a crack. A completely beautiful glass piece, so he wasn't so good at that. Sometimes it didn't go the way he wanted, let me put it that way.

42
01:07:33.530 --> 01:07:53.530
The greatest happiness in his life was, 25 years ago, when Beau was brought into the world. The first second Beau was born, he was sold. He often said to me, Beau is the best thing that ever happened to me.

43
01:07:54.530 --> 01:08:03.530
Bel was very worried at first about your hairstyle, because you were bald. Then she said, beautiful baby and nice, but she's as bald as can be.

44
01:08:04.530 --> 01:08:13.530
A little later I ran into him, and he was of course racing around everywhere with Beau, selling a car or this or that.

45
01:08:13.530 --> 01:08:27.530
Then you were sitting next to him in the car with your little legs, which just barely didn't reach over the seat, but with two little pigtails. And he was really proud of it, like look, she has pigtails.

46
01:08:27.530 --> 01:08:49.530
That pride has always remained. He always held you on a very high pedestal and no one ever reached that, except for your children.

47
01:08:50.530 --> 01:09:04.530
You really need to know that. He always spoke so proudly of you, how you did in school, how you worked through setbacks.

48
01:09:04.530 --> 01:09:31.530
Unfortunately, the relationship with Anita fell apart. I found it very sad. And despite the divorce, which didn't go so smoothly, he always said that you gave Anita the greatest gift that a man can give to people.

49
01:09:32.530 --> 01:09:46.530
When Jay and Led were born, your contact was restored. He was very grateful for that. He also indicated several times at that time that the way you separated should never have happened.

50
01:09:46.530 --> 01:10:03.530
And that you actually had it very good together, especially in the beginning. That you helped Anita with the bumpy road you traveled with so much dedication in the last months, he was really very happy about that.

51
01:10:03.530 --> 01:10:24.530
He told me several months, Anita is so good to me, it's really not normal, so good. The relationships he had after you were quite variable. I saw those girlfriends once in a fleeting moment, but that's all.

52
01:10:25.530 --> 01:10:45.530
Often he also rushed into those relationships. He wanted them too quickly and too much, and he indeed wanted to care, which sometimes felt very suffocating for that woman. He also wanted to put his own spin on it, so he wanted to change someone quite quickly, with the best intentions, with a good heart. But not everyone is waiting for that.

53
01:10:48.530 --> 01:10:51.530
So yes, that gave him a lot of unrest.

54
01:10:55.530 --> 01:11:13.530
If there had to be negotiations, you had to call Paul, then he could do it like no one else. Then you knew for sure you had a good deal.

55
01:11:13.530 --> 01:11:40.530
Paul was not afraid of dying. He knew that someone would be waiting for him. He had a lot of faith in God. That indeed brought him a lot of peace since he came here to the church. Before that, Paul was quite black-and-white, and when he came here to the church, he became a bit milder towards others and more accessible.

56
01:11:43.530 --> 01:11:59.530
Yes, it brought him a lot. But what he worried about a lot was, how will it be with Bo when I'm no longer there? Because he worried a lot about that. She's so young and with the children and this and that.

57
01:12:00.530 --> 01:12:23.530
Then I reminded him that just like you, Bo, I also lost my father at the age of 25. My father was also my rock in treatment. And I made it through, and you will make it through too, with the lovely people around you and your strong character and perseverance.

58
01:12:23.530 --> 01:12:51.530
It will go with ups and downs, but you will get there. He also worried about his grandchildren, because yes, I don't see my grandchildren growing up now, and I had no answer to that. But I do believe that there is something here. I have lost many people and dogs, and I still believe that they are always with me and support me where needed.

59
01:12:54.530 --> 01:12:56.530
Well, I think he will come to terms with that.

60
01:12:56.530 --> 01:13:21.530
Paul, my very best friend. We were different in so many ways. He was always too early, I was always too late. He loves people, I love dogs. Sometimes we didn't see each other for months, sometimes for years.

61
01:13:21.530 --> 01:13:49.530
And yet, when he stood at the door again, it was as if we had spoken just last week. The moments when I needed him, he was always there, immediately. If someone had passed away, well, he was right there on the doorstep. My friend had died, I hadn't seen him for a while, and there he was with broken arms at the door, even though I hadn't even messaged him.

62
01:13:51.530 --> 01:14:06.530
He was always there. My family was really crazy about Paul. I didn't understand at all why we had nothing, but we knew better, Paul and I. Yes, that never worked out. But that friendship was really very close.

63
01:14:06.530 --> 01:14:33.530
I am proud and grateful that you have been in my life, Paul. And I thank you for all the beautiful moments we shared together. I am grateful for the support you gave me when I needed it so badly. And I am very thankful, especially thankful that I could always be the same with you.

64
01:14:34.530 --> 01:14:40.530
Finally, I want to present you with a dilemma.

65
01:14:41.530 --> 01:15:05.530
Choice. You can cry because he is gone, or you can smile that he was there. You can pray that he comes back, or you can enjoy what he left behind. You can let your heart be empty because he is no longer there, or you can let your heart be full with what you shared.

66
01:15:06.530 --> 01:15:22.530
You can turn your back on tomorrow because of yesterday, or you can be happy with tomorrow because there was a yesterday. You can remember him and only that he is gone, or you can cherish your memories and let them live on.

67
01:15:22.530 --> 01:15:47.530
You can cry, shut yourself off, be empty and indifferent, or you can be the one you would immediately recognize. You can do it all or not do it, or you can do it all or not at all. You can tell yourself that you never wanted to experience this, or you can tell yourself that you never wanted to miss this.

68
01:15:47.530 --> 01:15:54.530
I choose the second option. Take care, Paul. Goodbye.

69
01:16:18.530 --> 01:16:23.530
Dear dad, there are so many memories that I will always look back on with a smile.

70
01:16:28.530 --> 01:16:37.530
Like that one time I found a letter in my shoe from Sinterklaas saying that we were going to Istanbul. I asked when? Tomorrow?

71
01:16:38.530 --> 01:17:03.530
During school time we went to Istanbul, but you had forgotten my sick notes at school and the school had already called and we were already at the airport. You cooked every day and loved food very much. That one time you said dinner was ready, you came walking in with more than a kilo of bacon. We ate that in front of the TV.

72
01:17:04.530 --> 01:17:26.530
What I will always remember are the moments we danced together in the living room. As a child, I would stand on your feet and we would dance around the room together. To the song by Marco Borsato's daughters. I will play that song especially for you, dad.

73
01:17:26.530 --> 01:17:48.530
Dad, how I will miss you. You were always my great example. If I had something on my mind, I would call you. And who is going to do all the chores around the house now? I will miss you, dad. I love you.

74
01:17:57.530 --> 01:18:21.530
A quarter past seven on Sunday morning, I hear a voice softly asking me. Are you awake yet, dad? Are you coming down with me? Do you have to work later or are you free today?

75
01:18:22.530 --> 01:18:47.530
Are you going to start with me? Oh, how time flies. Just yesterday I saw her for the first time, she was here in my arms. How beautiful she is and how well time suits her.

76
01:18:48.530 --> 01:19:03.530
I blink my eyes and see how she has changed a little each time. But no matter how big she may be, in my eyes she will always remain small.

77
01:19:04.530 --> 01:19:28.530
A quarter past seven on Sunday morning, I hear the front door quietly open. Now I can safely fall asleep. She is home. I had gone to gather a lot of love, but she had asked me not to stand there.

78
01:19:29.530 --> 01:19:54.530
She is now finding her way home. Oh, how time flies. Just yesterday I saw her for the first time, she was here in my arms. How beautiful she is and how well time suits her.

79
01:19:55.530 --> 01:20:10.530
I blink my eyes and see how she has changed a little each time. But no matter how big she may be, in my eyes she will always remain small.

80
01:20:10.530 --> 01:20:39.530
And sometimes, when I close my eyes, we walk together on the beach, her little hand in mine. And then she pauses time for a moment. It is just like it was then and she needs me again.

81
01:20:41.530 --> 01:20:52.530
I hold her close, just as she was. I hold her close.

82
01:20:52.530 --> 01:21:09.530
Quarter past seven on Sunday morning, she hears my voice gently waking her up. Today is your big day.

83
01:21:23.530 --> 01:21:48.530
How beautiful she is and how well time suits her. I blink my eyes and see how her hand is now full of another's. But wherever she may be, in thought she is here with me.

84
01:21:49.530 --> 01:21:59.530
In my eyes, she will always remain small.

85
01:21:59.530 --> 01:22:28.530
What a beautiful memories we could recall during this thanksgiving service. And on behalf of the family, I want to thank you all for being here to share in the farewell.

86
01:22:30.530 --> 01:22:38.530
And also to everyone watching, thank you for taking the time to watch and in that way say goodbye.

87
01:22:39.530 --> 01:22:47.530
Soon Tim will conclude the service, after which we will watch a presentation that Bo made herself.

88
01:22:48.530 --> 01:22:53.530
And after that presentation, everyone will have the opportunity to say goodbye to Paul.

89
01:22:53.530 --> 01:23:07.530
The family would like to be the last to leave the room. After that, the family will go to the crematorium with Paul and say goodbye to him in private.

90
01:23:08.530 --> 01:23:12.530
I want to give the word to Tim.

91
01:23:13.530 --> 01:23:15.530
Can we perhaps stand for a moment?

92
01:23:20.530 --> 01:23:24.530
I don't know if you are religious or if you believe in God.

93
01:23:25.530 --> 01:23:30.530
Only Paul did, and I want to invite us all to close our eyes.

94
01:23:31.530 --> 01:23:40.530
In any case, close your eyes out of respect for Paul's faith or close your eyes in faith in God.

95
01:23:41.530 --> 01:23:46.530
But I will pray now. Father in heaven, I thank you for the life of Paul.

96
01:23:47.530 --> 01:23:50.530
Lord, I thank you that everything he leaves behind was a blessing.

97
01:23:51.530 --> 01:23:57.530
Lord, I thank you that he leaves behind a wonderful daughter with beautiful grandchildren.

98
01:23:58.530 --> 01:24:04.530
On the words we just heard, that Paul was worried about, I want to ask you, Father in heaven.

99
01:24:04.530 --> 01:24:11.530
That you will provide for every need in the life of Beau and Beau and the grandchildren.

100
01:24:12.530 --> 01:24:16.530
Father, that it will go well with them. That they will have hope for the future.

101
01:24:17.530 --> 01:24:20.530
Lord, I want to pray for everyone in this room that you are good to us.

102
01:24:21.530 --> 01:24:25.530
Lord, that you will show hope in these days, days of pain.

103
01:24:26.530 --> 01:24:29.530
But even in days of pain, you can bring peace, you are the expert in that.

104
01:24:30.530 --> 01:24:32.530
Lord, I want to thank you that you are a God of hope.

105
01:24:32.530 --> 01:24:35.530
I bless everyone in this room, in Jesus' name.

106
01:24:36.530 --> 01:24:40.530
That you will know sooner or later that there is a God who loves us. Amen.

